/* std::iter -- the Iter family (10-iteration.md): two traits, the
 * sequence's own shape. Iter is the loop's own word -- the
 * associated type says what it yields, the one method advances and
 * yields it, None the sequence's end. IntoIter turns a container
 * into its iterator, consuming it; an iterator is its own IntoIter,
 * so the blanket row hands every Iter back.
 *
 * for-in rides both: the sugar spells c.into_iter() once, then
 * it.next() a round, the answer matched -- Some runs the body, None
 * ends the loop, exactly the desugar 10-iteration.md writes. What
 * the loop does not take destructs with the iterator itself at the
 * loop's exit, its fields the walk's own recursion (03-move.md).
 *
 * An owned array's ArrayIter waits on the one thing it needs: an
 * impl's own const length, [N]T routed through the impl table
 * (08-reflection.md). Until then an array iterates the compiler's
 * own special case, element by element (10). */
pub trait Iter {
  type Item;
  fn next(self: *mut Self) -> ?Self::Item;   /* None ends the sequence */
}

pub trait IntoIter {
  type Iter;
  fn into_iter(self: Self) -> Self::Iter;
}

impl<I: Iter> IntoIter for I {
  type Iter = I;
  fn into_iter(self: Self) -> I { self }
}
